## HikariCP Configuration in Spring Boot

```yaml
# application.yml
spring:
  datasource:
    url: jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/mydb
    username: myuser
    password: mypassword
    hikari:
      # === CORE SETTINGS ===
      maximum-pool-size: 10       # Max connections in pool
                                   # Rule: (cores × 2) + disk spindles
                                   # 4 cores + SSD → 9-10
      minimum-idle: 5             # Keep 5 connections warm at all times
                                   # Set = maximumPoolSize to disable idle eviction

      # === TIMEOUT SETTINGS ===
      connection-timeout: 30000   # 30s: max wait for a connection from pool
                                   # Throws exception if no connection available
      idle-timeout: 600000        # 10min: evict connections idle longer than this
      max-lifetime: 1800000       # 30min: replace connections older than this
                                   # Set < DB's wait_timeout (MySQL) or
                                   # tcp_keepalives (PostgreSQL)
      keepalive-time: 30000       # 30s: send keepalive ping to prevent timeout

      # === HEALTH SETTINGS ===
      validation-timeout: 5000    # 5s: timeout for connection validation query
      connection-test-query: "SELECT 1"  # Test query (auto-detected for most DBs)

      # === METADATA ===
      pool-name: MainPool         # Name for JMX/logging
      register-mbeans: true       # Enable JMX monitoring
```

## Pool Sizing — The Formula

```
PGBouncer (PostgreSQL team) recommendation:
  pool_size = num_cores × 2 + effective_io_concurrency

For a 4-core server with SSD (io_concurrency=1):
  pool_size = 4 × 2 + 1 = 9 → use 10

For a 8-core server with SSD:
  pool_size = 8 × 2 + 1 = 17 → use 20

Intuition:
  When a thread is waiting on I/O (disk read, network), the CPU is free.
  × 2 accounts for the CPU being useful while I/O completes.
  Beyond ~2× cores, you hit diminishing returns then throughput DROPS.

  Experiment: measure throughput with pool_size = 5, 10, 20, 50, 100.
  You'll often find 10 outperforms 100 because less DB context switching.

MULTIPLE APP INSTANCES:
  If you run 10 instances of your app, each with pool_size=10:
  → 100 connections to DB
  → DB sees 100 concurrent connections
  → Configure DB's max_connections accordingly
  → Use PgBouncer as connection proxy if needed (pool at DB level)
```

## Connection Lifecycle

```java
// What happens internally when you call:
@Transactional
public Order createOrder(CreateOrderRequest request) {
    // 1. Transaction starts → HikariCP provides connection from pool
    //    (microseconds if connection available, up to connectionTimeout if pool exhausted)

    Order order = orderRepository.save(buildOrder(request));
    // 2. SQL executed on the borrowed connection

    return order;
    // 3. Transaction commits → connection RETURNED to pool (not closed)
    //    Pool resets connection state (auto-commit, isolation level, etc.)
    //    Connection is ready for next borrower
}
// Actual TCP connection to DB stays open and is reused
// No TCP handshake, no auth — just execute SQL directly

// If pool is exhausted:
// Thread 100 requests connection → pool has 0 available → waits up to 30s
// If no connection freed within 30s → SQLTransientConnectionException thrown
// Solution: increase pool size OR find query taking too long (holding connection)
```

## Monitoring Connection Pool Health

```java
// Via Spring Boot Actuator:
// GET /actuator/metrics/hikaricp.connections.active
// GET /actuator/metrics/hikaricp.connections.idle
// GET /actuator/metrics/hikaricp.connections.pending  ← critical metric!
// GET /actuator/metrics/hikaricp.connections.timeout  ← alert if > 0

// Prometheus metrics (if micrometer-registry-prometheus added):
// hikaricp_connections_active
// hikaricp_connections_idle
// hikaricp_connections_pending  ← Alert: threads waiting for connection
// hikaricp_connections_timeout_total  ← Alert: connections timed out
// hikaricp_connection_acquire_seconds  ← How long to get connection

// Set up alerts:
//   pending > 0 for > 30s → pool undersized or slow queries holding connections
//   timeout_total increases → immediate action needed

// JMX monitoring (if register-mbeans: true):
// Connect VisualVM or JConsole to see pool stats in real time
```

## Common Pool Exhaustion Scenarios

```
SCENARIO 1: Slow query holding connection
  Thread A acquires connection → runs 30-second report query
  30 threads × 30-second queries = pool exhausted for 30 seconds
  FIX: Add query timeout; run reports via dedicated read replica

SCENARIO 2: Connection leak
  Code acquires connection but throws before returning it
  FIX: Use try-with-resources / @Transactional / Spring template
  DETECT: leak-detection-threshold: 2000  # Warn if connection held > 2s

SCENARIO 3: N+1 queries in loop
  for (Order o : orders) { o.getUser().getName(); }
  100 orders × 1 user query = 100 connections needed simultaneously
  FIX: Eager fetch or JOIN FETCH

SCENARIO 4: Undersized pool for traffic spike
  Normal load: 3 concurrent queries → pool-size=5 is fine
  Traffic spike: 50 concurrent queries → 45 threads waiting
  FIX: Increase pool-size, add connection queue monitoring
```

## Production Reality

Start with `maximum-pool-size: 10` and monitor. Set `leak-detection-threshold: 2000` in production — any connection held for more than 2 seconds is likely a leak or slow query worth investigating. Configure `max-lifetime` to be less than your database's connection timeout setting to avoid getting dead connections from the pool. For PostgreSQL, the default `tcp_keepalives_idle` is 2 hours — set `max-lifetime` to 1800000ms (30 minutes) to stay well under that.
